        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Are the Different Types of Wood Filler?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are five: one-part latex or acrylic, one-part solvent-based, two-part polyester, two-part epoxy paste and epoxy putty sticks. The shelf looks crowded, but nearly every product falls into one of those groups, and each group has jobs it handles well and jobs it should never be given. Work out which group you need before you compare brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One-part latex or acrylic</h3>

          <p>
            These are the familiar ready-to-use tubs. They clean up with water and sand easily. Because they harden by giving up water, they shrink slightly as they dry and work best in shallow holes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One-part solvent-based</h3>

          <p>
            These older formulas harden as their solvent evaporates. Most are tougher than latex fillers, but they smell stronger and need solvent for cleanup, so open a window while you work. Water-based products have replaced many of them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-part polyester</h3>

          <p>
            A filler is mixed with a hardener just before use; Minwax High Performance is the common example. The result sets fast, does not shrink and is strong enough for exterior trim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-part epoxy paste and epoxy putty sticks</h3>

          <p>
            Epoxy pastes such as Abatron WoodEpox are the heavy-duty choice for rot repair and for rebuilding wood that is missing altogether. Epoxy putty sticks such as KwikWood package the same chemistry in a convenient form for small, strong repairs.
          </p>

