        <div id="filler-putty-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is the Difference Between Wood Filler, Wood Putty and Caulk?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filler dries hard, putty stays soft and caulk stays flexible, so each belongs in a different place. People use the three names as if they were interchangeable, and that mix-up sits behind a large share of failed repairs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood filler hardens</h3>

          <p>
            Filler goes onto bare wood, gets sanded flush and is then painted or finished. Since it is rigid once dry, it belongs in damage that does not move: nail holes, gouges, dents and chipped edges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood putty stays soft</h3>

          <p>
            Putty is usually oil-based, comes in wood tones and goes into nail holes after the final finish is already on, typically on stained trim and furniture. It is never sanded, and it is not meant for bare wood that will be painted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Caulk stays flexible</h3>

          <p>
            Caulk is for the seams where two separate pieces shift against each other, such as where trim meets a wall or siding, or where a window casing meets its frame. A rigid filler in one of those seams will crack with the first change of season. The reverse mistake is just as visible: caulk smeared into a nail hole on a flat board stays soft, settles a little and shows through the paint. For outdoor seams, our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> guide has the picks.
          </p>

