          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When do you need all three?</h3>

          <p>
            Repainting exterior window trim is a good example, because it usually needs every one of them. Filler goes into the nail holes and dents in the casing, followed by sanding and primer. Epoxy rebuilds any part of the sill that has gone soft. An elastomeric sealant then closes the seams between casing and siding, and our <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">exterior window caulking guide</a> walks through that part. Each material handles only the task it was designed for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Stainable Wood Filler Really Stainable?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Partly. Stainable filler absorbs some stain and changes color, but it almost never ends up looking like the wood around it. Stainable is the most overused word on filler labels, because real wood has grain, uneven density and thirsty end grain, and a uniform paste cannot copy any of that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you match the filler to the stain?</h3>

          <p>
            Pick a filler tone close to the final stained color rather than the bare wood. Apply it, let it dry, sand it, then stain a test board that carries both wood and filler. A patch that comes out too light calls for a darker filler, or for a touch of the stain or a compatible tint mixed into the filler before applying; one that comes out too dark calls for a lighter tone. Minwax Stainable and Goodfilla are the easiest products to adjust this way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you skip filler under the stain?</h3>

          <p>
            Often, yes. Most finish carpenters skip filler under stain when they work on trim. They stain and topcoat the wood first, then press colored wax or an oil-based putty, chosen to match the finished color, into the nail holes. On small holes that approach disappears far more convincingly than any stainable filler.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE FOR STAIN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Test on scrap, every time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Make the sample from the same species, filler and stain as the real job, and look at it under the light in the room where the piece will live. A ten-minute test is far cheaper than a glaring patch in the center of a tabletop.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can two-part fillers be stained?</h3>

          <p>
            Not in any meaningful way. Two-part fillers and epoxies are meant to be painted over. If the job is stained exterior wood, like a cedar fence or a stained entry door, you will either live with a repair that shows or swap in a new board. Decks and siding under semi-transparent stain have a few more options, which our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain guide</a> covers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is the Best Wood Filler for Exterior and Rotted Wood?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For small holes outdoors, an exterior-rated latex such as DAP Plastic Wood-X works once it is primed and painted. Anything bigger than a nail hole on exterior trim, siding or a door is better served by a two-part product, and rot needs a consolidant followed by an epoxy such as Abatron WoodEpox. Outside, filler has to survive sun, water and seasonal movement, and exterior latex is vulnerable when it is left bare or used in large patches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you repair rotted wood?</h3>

          <p>
            Rotted wood has lost its structure, so filler laid straight over it has nothing solid to grip. The repair takes four steps:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><strong>Remove the rot.</strong> Cut out every bit of wood soft enough to dent with a fingernail or probe with an awl, using a chisel, rasp or oscillating tool.</li>
            <li><strong>Let it dry.</strong> Give the area time to dry out thoroughly, because moisture trapped under epoxy causes the repair to fail.</li>
            <li><strong>Consolidate.</strong> Soak the remaining fibers with a consolidant such as Abatron LiquidWood or Minwax Wood Hardener, which penetrates and hardens them.</li>
            <li><strong>Rebuild.</strong> Fill with a two-part epoxy or polyester filler, overfilling slightly and shaping it close to the final profile while it is still workable. Once it has cured, sand or plane it flush, prime and paint.</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            Done properly, a repair like this can last as long as the wood around it. Done over damp or punky wood, it pops off within a year. Mix and apply two-part products with good ventilation, since some, Minwax High Performance among them, carry a strong odor, and wear gloves while handling uncured epoxy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you replace the wood instead?</h3>

          <p>
            Replacement usually wins when the rot goes through most of the board's thickness, touches a structural member, or covers more than roughly a third of a trim piece. At that point new wood is often faster to install and outlasts a large patch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why fix the water first?</h3>

          <p>
            Because the rot comes back if the water does. At a window sill the usual cause is water slipping past failed caulk or a missing drip cap; at the bottom of a door jamb it is often splashback or a door with no sweep. Once the repair is done, seal the joints around it with exterior sealant and keep the wood covered with sound paint or a finish from our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a>. Filler does nothing to stop water; it only fills the space water has already rotted away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Apply and Sand Wood Filler?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the hole, press the filler in with a slight crown, build deep holes in layers, sand flat once it is fully dry, and prime before painting. Each step below explains why it matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean the hole</h3>

          <p>
            Pick out loose fibers, dust and any old finish, since filler only bonds well to sound wood. A putty knife corner is enough for small holes. With bigger damage, cut the edges square with a chisel so the filler has firm walls to lock against. Wipe the dust off with a rag that is barely damp, then let the wood dry before filling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Press, do not spread</h3>

          <p>
            Force filler into the hole with a flexible putty knife, then scrape across at a low angle to take off most of the excess. Aim for a slight crown above the surface, since one-part fillers shrink a little as they dry. Too tall a crown only means extra sanding; a fill that sits flush or low will need a second coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Build deep holes in layers</h3>

          <p>
            Keep each layer of one-part latex filler to a quarter inch or less. A deeper plug forms a dry crust over a wet center, and when that center finally dries it shrinks and cracks. Put in one layer, wait until it is dry all the way through, then add another. Two-part fillers and epoxies tolerate thicker fills, but only mix an amount you can apply before the label's working time runs out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Sand once fully dry</h3>

          <p>
            On flat work, begin with 120-grit wrapped on a sanding block so the patch ends up level rather than scooped out, then move to 180- or 220-grit. For molded trim, wrap the paper around a dowel or a profile block that fits the shape. Dust off, shine a raking light across the surface to find pinholes and low spots, and fill those before moving on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Prime before painting</h3>

          <p>
            Wood and filler absorb paint at different rates, so an unprimed patch flashes, showing as a dull or glossy spot through the topcoat. A dab of primer over each patch stops that. Outdoors, use a good bonding primer, and get the paint on within the time window given on the filler and primer labels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long Does Wood Filler Take to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anywhere from about 15 minutes for a thin latex skim to as long as a day for a two-part epoxy paste, depending on the type, the depth and the temperature. Drying time is one of the biggest practical gaps between filler types, and it often decides which one people grab. The ranges below are typical; the label on your product always wins.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Times Before Sanding</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15-60 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin latex fill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Deep latex fill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Two-part polyester</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Hours to a day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Two-part epoxy paste</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does latex filler take longer in deep holes?</h3>

          <p>
            Latex filler dries as its water evaporates, so the depth of the fill, the temperature and the humidity set the pace. A thin skim on a nail hole can be ready for sandpaper in 15 minutes, a quarter-inch fill can need a few hours, and a deep hole packed in one go can take much longer and may stay soft at its core. Color-change formulas such as DAP Plastic Wood-X take out the guesswork, because the shift in color tells you it is dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How fast do two-part fillers cure?</h3>

          <p>
            Two-part products harden through a chemical reaction instead of evaporation, which means the center of the patch cures along with the surface. Polyester fillers are generally the fastest and can often be sanded within half an hour. Epoxy pastes take longer: they stay workable long enough to shape, then need anywhere from several hours to overnight to cure, depending on temperature. Cold weather slows every two-part product considerably.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">In what order should you fill, caulk and paint?</h3>

          <p>
            If the trim needs caulk too, sequence the work so nothing is rushed. Do the filling, sanding and priming first, run the caulk second, and hold off on paint until the sealant's label says it can take paint. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">caulk drying times</a> explains that last wait.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Can You Screw or Nail Into Wood Filler?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not into one-part latex, but a cured two-part epoxy or epoxy putty stick can take a screw through a pilot hole. Latex and acrylic fillers were never designed to grip fasteners: drive a screw into one and the filler usually breaks up while the screw turns without catching. For a stripped hinge screw, reach for something else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can epoxy hold a screw?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, after a full cure. Pack the stripped hole, wait for the cure, then drill a pilot hole to suit the screw and drive it home. J-B Weld and Abatron both describe their products as drillable and able to take fasteners once cured.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the best fix for a heavily loaded screw?</h3>

          <p>
            When the fastener carries real load, such as a door hinge or a railing bracket, the old-school repair still holds up best. Glue a hardwood dowel, or a few wooden toothpicks or matchsticks, into the hole, cut them off flush once dry and drill a fresh pilot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you nail through filler?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually not. Driving a nail through most fillers cracks them, though a cured epoxy paste will generally accept a nail through a predrilled hole. If you know the repair will need fastening, decide before you fill: choose an epoxy, or bed a wood block or dowel in the repair to nail into later.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will filler restore structural strength?</h3>

          <p>
            No. These are surface and trim repairs, and none of them puts real strength back into a framing member, a stair tread or any part that carries load. Structural damage calls for replacement or reinforcement, and anything bearing weight above your head or under your feet should be properly assessed before filler enters the picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much Does Wood Filler Cost, and How Long Does It Keep?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, roughly $5 to $15 for a small to pint-size tub of one-part latex or acrylic, about $15 to $25 for a two-part polyester kit, roughly $8 to $15 for each epoxy putty stick, and $40 to $80 or more for a full two-part epoxy repair system with consolidant. Next to the time a repair takes, filler is cheap. How long it keeps depends mostly on keeping air out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What size should you buy?</h3>

          <p>
            Buy only what you expect to use over the next year. Once air gets into the container, one-part fillers start to harden, and the fast-drying kinds harden fastest, so a pint that dries out half used costs more than the small tub would have. Wipe the rim so the lid closes tight, lay plastic wrap on the surface before sealing it, and keep the container upside down somewhere that stays mild. Freezing ruins most water-based fillers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long do two-part fillers keep?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually longer. The two components do nothing until they are combined, although hardener can lose strength with age and heat, so mark the purchase date on the can. Epoxy putty sticks last well if you wrap them tightly after each use. Goodfilla, being water-based, is the odd one out among tubs: if it dries out, adding water brings it back, which saves waste for anyone who only fills a few holes a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where is it worth spending more?</h3>

          <p>
            A cheap latex filler is fine for painted nail holes. On stained furniture, a few extra dollars for a stainable formula in the right color pays back in looks. For exterior rot, a proper epoxy system costs far less than replacement millwork plus a carpenter's time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Which Wood Filler Is Best for Your Project?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the product to the project: latex for painted holes, a stainable formula or Goodfilla for stained wood, a two-part product for exterior damage, epoxy for rot, and caulk rather than filler for joints that move.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nail holes and dents in painted trim</h3>

          <p>
            A one-part latex such as DAP Plastic Wood-X or Gorilla All Purpose covers it. With a lot of holes and a same-day paint deadline, FamoWood's faster drying helps. Prime before painting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stained furniture, cabinets and floors</h3>

          <p>
            Use Minwax Stainable or Goodfilla in a tone close to the final finish, and test on scrap first. On trim that is already finished, colored putty applied afterward can beat filler applied before.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Woodworking and open-grain species</h3>

          <p>
            Goodfilla's ability to double as a grain filler is a real advantage on furniture projects in open-pored woods such as oak and ash.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exterior trim, doors and siding</h3>

          <p>
            For damage beyond a nail hole, use a two-part polyester such as Minwax High Performance for fast paint-grade repairs, or KwikWood for small, strong fixes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotted sills, jambs and columns</h3>

          <p>
            Harden the remaining fibers with a consolidant, rebuild the missing wood with Abatron WoodEpox, and track down the leak that started the rot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joints between two moving pieces</h3>

          <p>
            Set the filler down and pick up a tube of exterior sealant. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk rankings</a> show which sealant suits each kind of joint. Filler repairs holes and caulk seals gaps; keep the two straight and both will last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Wood Filler
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the word stainable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stainable on the label means the patch will absorb some color, not that it will blend into the grain, and many patches end up lighter or blotchier than the surrounding wood. Start with a filler tone near the finished color, run a scrap test with the actual stain, and for small holes consider colored putty applied after the finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a deep hole in one pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick plug of one-part latex skins over on top while the core stays soft, then shrinks and cracks as that core dries. Build the fill in layers of about a quarter inch, letting each one dry completely, or switch to a two-part product that hardens all the way through without shrinking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using filler in a joint that moves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid filler between trim and wall, or between casing and siding, cracks as soon as humidity and temperature shift the pieces. Moving joints need a flexible sealant. Keep filler for holes and damage inside a single piece of wood, and run caulk along the joints where two pieces meet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Patching over rot without removing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filler spread over soft, rotted wood has nothing sound to grip and seals moisture in underneath, so the patch pops loose while the rot keeps spreading. Dig out every soft fiber, let the area dry, harden what remains with a consolidant, rebuild with epoxy and fix the water source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving exterior filler unprimed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exterior-rated latex filler still degrades when sun and rain hit it bare, and because it soaks up paint differently from wood, an unprimed patch shows as flashing. Spot-prime every repair with a bonding primer and then paint the whole surface. With primer and paint over them, small outdoor fills last much longer; inspect them each spring.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which filler should I use on outdoor trim and siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On painted exterior wood with small holes, DAP Plastic Wood-X holds up well once it is primed and painted. Larger damage, broken edges and weathered trim call for a two-part product such as Minwax High Performance or Abatron WoodEpox, which lasts longer because it does not shrink and resists moisture. Rotted wood needs a consolidant first, then an epoxy filler.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is wood putty the same as wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, they are different products. Filler hardens; it goes onto bare wood, is sanded flush and is then painted or finished. Putty stays soft, is usually oil-based and fills nail holes after the finish is already on, mostly on stained work. Putty is never sanded, and it should not be used on bare wood that is going to be painted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I make wood filler match stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a filler shade that matches the finished, stained color rather than the bare wood. Once it is applied, dry and sanded, stain a sample board carrying both the wood and the filler, and change the filler shade if the patch reads wrong. Products built to take stain, Minwax Stainable and Goodfilla among them, are easier to fine-tune than all-purpose tubs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can sand wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A thin latex fill often sands in 15 minutes to an hour, while a deep latex fill can need several hours or more. Two-part polyester fillers usually sand within about half an hour, and epoxy pastes may need several hours to overnight. Depth and temperature change everything, so follow the label and confirm the center is hard, not just the skin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fill a big hole or a missing chunk of wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reach for a two-part product instead of a one-part latex. Polyester fillers and epoxy pastes cure all the way through without shrinking and can be built up thick. For a very large void, glue in a block of wood to take up most of the space and fill around it. In rotted areas, remove all soft wood and apply a consolidant before filling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a screw hold in wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in one-part latex filler; it crumbles as the screw bites. A cured two-part epoxy or epoxy putty stick can hold one, provided you drill a pilot hole once it has cured. For stripped hinge screws and other fasteners under load, the most dependable fix is still to glue a hardwood dowel or wood slivers into the hole and drill it again.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can wood filler be used on decks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for small cosmetic repairs on painted or solid-stained decks, and even then the patch tends to show. Deck boards move with moisture, carry foot traffic and usually wear semi-transparent stain that will not hide filler, so badly damaged boards are better replaced. Save epoxy for small, localized damage on railings or trim.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

